      "status": "mathematical proof reconstructed",
      "correspondence": "Use the 2M-dimensional trace quadratic form over F and a positive Gaussian majorant. The weighted discrepancy of the theta and Eisenstein families is uniformly bounded by Siegel reduction and the supplied estimates. Dilating the finite lattice gives archimedean weight a^(−dM). Integrating the discrepancy against the frequency character on an additive fundamental domain extracts the single coefficient. The two coefficient formulas identify the average with I(a) and its reference value with wP(a).",
      "review": "Checked the positive and finite integral and the exponent of the undone weight at the actual call sites. The bound makes no claim that I(a) equals wP(a) before the scalar has been determined."
    },
    {
      "id": "OVM-02",
      "chapter": "lifts",
      "title": "Local scaling gives a positive product of the required size on a subsequence",
      "anchor": "lifts-one-vector-growth",
      "statement": "There is a κ>0 and an unbounded sequence a=Np^k such that the product P(a) of finite-place Gram-fibre masses on a⁻¹Λ is at least κ a^{d(2M−2)}. At v the dilation contributes q_v^{ord_v(a)(2M−2)} times the standard-lattice mass at a²β.",
      "hypotheses": "M≥3, every finite local fibre measure at β is nonzero, and the local Gram measures and Haar normalizations obey the scaling formula. Good-place estimates apply to nonzero integral targets. Fix N so that N²β is integral and sufficient lattice dilations capture positive mass at every exceptional place.",
      "equations": [
        "lifts-one-vector-growth-bound"
      ],
