      "status": "mathematical proof reconstructed",
      "correspondence": "Choose p=1+∏q_v over the finite exceptional set. At places away from p, scaling by the local unit p^k leaves the standard-lattice mass at N²β unchanged. These masses are positive and their deviations from one are absolutely summable. Every place over p is good, and there the uniform lower bound is 1/2. The finite-place product of the explicit valuation factors equals a^{d(2M−2)}.",
      "review": "Checked the dependence on N and p, the absence of any primality requirement on the integer p and the unbounded sequence. For the inert prime 3 and the standard ternary Hermitian form the mod-3 counts give 252 vectors of norm one and 224 nonzero ones of norm zero. At target nine the local density is 2044/2187; dilation of the test gives 2044/27."
    },
    {
      "id": "OVM-03",
      "chapter": "lifts",
      "title": "Growth against the discrepancy fixes the normalization of the genus measure",
      "anchor": "lifts-one-vector-constant",
      "statement": "The one-vector genus-average measure equals the compatible product of Gram-fibre measures with scalar one. When every fibre measure is nonzero, evaluation on the same tests gives I(a)=c wP(a). The two estimates imply |c−1|≤C/(wκ)a^{−d(M−2)} on an unbounded sequence, hence c=1. When a fibre measure is zero the genus measure and the product vanish and the equality already holds with scalar one.",
      "hypotheses": "Apply the invariant factorization to a nondegenerate one-by-one Hermitian target, identify it with a nonzero scalar β of F, and assume M≥3. The exact formula uses the archimedean test and the finite lattice characteristic functions from OVM-01, with the standard lattice unchanged at almost all places.",
